{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Instructor Toolkit for CAT 531","title":"Readiness Review","description":"CAT 531 textbook section on running a readiness review as a go/no-go launch gate: assigning traceable verdicts (go, no-go, conditional) to each prior toolkit artifact, holding a no-go on a safety, consent, or equity blocker, and recording rejected boundary-reversing shortcuts.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"08","learning_objectives":["Run a readiness review as a go/no-go gate: assign a traceable verdict to each prior artifact, let a single safety, consent, or equity blocker hold the launch, and record a rejected boundary-reversing shortcut.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible."],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531","cat531_supplement_readiness_review","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/08/07"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_review_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_review_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_review_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_review_calibration"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false},"content":"# Readiness Review\n\n![Readiness Review textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/08-08-readiness-review.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nA readiness review is the gate a teacher passes through before deploying an AI tool with real students. The defining feature of a gate, as opposed to a celebration, is that it can return a no-go. A review that only confirms what everyone hopes is not a review; it is a rubber stamp. This closing section asks you to check each artifact the toolkit produced against a go or no-go criterion, to attach a verdict to each one, and to hold a no-go when the evidence requires it, even when launch is scheduled for next week.\n\nThis matters because the cost of a premature launch falls on students and families. Unsent consent forms, an uncollected baseline, a rubric that punishes accommodation: any one of these is enough to harm someone if the tool goes live anyway. A readiness review that names the failing item and blocks launch until it closes is the most consequential act of professional judgment in the whole toolkit.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Run a readiness review as a go/no-go gate: assign a traceable verdict to each prior artifact, let a single safety, consent, or equity blocker hold the launch, and record a rejected boundary-reversing shortcut.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA team is set to launch an AI tutor next week. The lead writes a readiness note: \"Everything looks good, we are ready.\" But consent forms have not gone home, and the pre-tool baseline has not been collected. The cheerful note has skipped the two items that should block launch. A real readiness review would have caught both, returned a no-go on consent, and a conditional on the baseline. The question this section answers is how to run the review as a gate that ties each verdict to a specific prior artifact and is willing to say not yet.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nA readiness review is a structured audit of the artifacts produced in earlier sections, each given one of three verdicts:\n\n- **Go** means the artifact meets its criterion and presents no blocker. The tension map names a real conflict and a bounded decision; the troubleshooting transcript shows edits and a principled rejection; the rubric scores judgment with its equity note.\n- **No-go** means an item is missing or unsafe and launch must wait. Unsent consent forms are a classic no-go: without them the tool is being used on students whose families never agreed, and no amount of enthusiasm overrides that.\n- **Conditional** means an item is acceptable but incomplete, with a defined action that must finish before or during launch. An evidence plan with a defined baseline that has not yet been collected is conditional: collect it before week one or the later result row is untestable.\n\nThe review's integrity depends on traceability: each verdict points to a specific prior artifact, so the verdict is checkable rather than impressionistic. It also depends on the willingness to reject a tempting shortcut. \"Launch now and gather consent afterward\" reverses the policy boundary and must be refused; recording that refusal is part of the review. Teacher professional judgment is the anchor that makes a no-go possible, because it locates the authority to halt a launch with the teacher rather than with the schedule.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare a review that rubber-stamps a launch with one that runs as an honest gate.\n\n### Annotated judgment call: a readiness review that is a gate, not a celebration\n\nScenario: at the end of the toolkit, a teacher runs a readiness review before deploying an AI tool in a real class. A weak review declares readiness; a strong review checks each prior-section artifact against a go or no-go criterion and is willing to return a no-go.\n\nBefore (a review that rubber-stamps):\n\n> Readiness review: Everything looks good. We are ready to launch the AI tutor with students next week.\n\nWalk the review as a gate, checking the artifacts the earlier sections produced:\n\n1. Tension named (08.01)? The studio map names the value conflict and a negotiated decision. Status: pass.\n2. Tool behavior bounded (08.02)? The TeachGen@i transcript shows teacher edits and at least one rejected suggestion. Status: pass.\n3. Equity and rubric (08.04)? The rubric scores judgment, not tool presence, with the risk note. Status: pass.\n4. Policy and fallback (08.06)? The memo answers privacy, consent, equity, and names a low-tech fallback. Status: fail. Consent forms have not gone home, so a no-go is required on that item.\n5. Evidence plan (08.07)? A baseline and a verifiable result row exist. Status: conditional, baseline not yet collected.\n\nAfter (annotated readiness review with an honest verdict):\n\n> Readiness review (08.08): GO on tension (08.01), tool boundary (08.02), and rubric (08.04). NO-GO on policy: consent forms are unsent, so launch is blocked until opt-out paths and the low-tech fallback from 08.06 are in place. CONDITIONAL on evidence: collect the pre-AI baseline before week 1 so the 08.07 result row is testable. Overall verdict: not ready next week; ready after the consent and baseline items close. I rejected the suggestion to launch and gather consent afterward because that reverses the policy boundary.\n\nThe after review is stronger because it treats readiness as a gate that can fail, ties each verdict to a specific prior artifact, and shows the judgment to hold a no-go rather than rubber-stamp a launch.\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"The readiness gate: go, no-go, or conditional\",\"start\":\"n1\",\"nodes\":{\"n1\":{\"prompt\":\"Launch is scheduled next week. The tension map (08.01), the troubleshooting transcript (08.02), and the rubric (08.04) all meet their criteria. But consent forms have not gone home and the pre-AI baseline has not been collected. What verdict structure do you use?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Average the verdicts: most items pass, so declare overall readiness.\",\"next\":\"average\",\"feedback\":\"Readiness is not a majority vote. A single consent failure blocks launch regardless of how many items are green.\"},{\"label\":\"Assign a verdict per artifact and let a single blocker hold the launch.\",\"next\":\"perItem\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. A gate works item by item, and one safety or consent blocker can stop everything.\"}]},\"average\":{\"prompt\":\"You declared readiness, but consent forms are still unsent. The tool launches on students whose families never agreed. What is the consequence?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"It is acceptable; consent can be gathered afterward.\",\"next\":\"shortcut\",\"feedback\":\"That reverses the policy boundary. Consent precedes use, not follows it.\"},{\"label\":\"Students are harmed by omission; the launch should have been blocked.\",\"next\":\"perItem\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Averaging hid a no-go that must stop the launch.\"}]},\"perItem\":{\"prompt\":\"You reach the consent item: forms are unsent. What verdict does this earn?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Conditional: launch and finish consent during week 1.\",\"next\":\"shortcut\",\"feedback\":\"Consent is not a during-launch task. Using the tool without it is the harm itself, so this is a no-go, not a conditional.\"},{\"label\":\"No-go: block launch until opt-out paths and the fallback are in place.\",\"next\":\"baseline\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Unsent consent is a classic no-go that halts the launch clock.\"}]},\"shortcut\":{\"prompt\":\"Someone proposes: ’Launch now, collect consent and baseline afterward, so we stay on schedule.’ What do you do?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Accept it to protect the timeline.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"This trades a real boundary for convenience. Launching before consent reverses the policy boundary.\"},{\"label\":\"Refuse it and record the refusal as part of the review.\",\"next\":\"baseline\",\"feedback\":\"Yes. Recording the rejected shortcut is part of the review integrity check.\"}]},\"baseline\":{\"prompt\":\"The evidence plan (08.07) has a defined baseline that has not yet been collected. What verdict fits, since the tool can run safely while you collect it?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"No-go, same as consent.\",\"next\":\"end_weak\",\"feedback\":\"Too severe. The baseline does not endanger students; it is acceptable but incomplete, so it is conditional with a deadline.\"},{\"label\":\"Conditional: collect the pre-AI baseline before week 1 so the later result row is testable.\",\"next\":\"end_strong\",\"feedback\":\"Correct. Conditional means acceptable but incomplete, with a defined action that must finish.\"}]},\"end_weak\":{\"prompt\":\"Result: the review rubber-stamped a launch or accepted a boundary-reversing shortcut, so students were exposed before consent or the gate lost its integrity.\",\"feedback\":\"A review that cannot return a no-go, or that averages away a blocker, is not protecting anyone.\",\"choices\":[]},\"end_strong\":{\"prompt\":\"Result: GO on tension, boundary, and rubric; NO-GO on consent; CONDITIONAL on baseline; overall verdict not ready until those items close, with the refused shortcut recorded.\",\"feedback\":\"This is a gate, not a celebration: traceable per-artifact verdicts and the judgment to hold a no-go.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Pull each prior-section artifact and judge it against its own criterion\", \"Assign go, no-go, or conditional to each, citing the section it came from\", \"Let any single safety, consent, or equity no-go hold the whole launch\", \"Refuse and record any shortcut that reverses a boundary (such as consent after launch)\", \"State an honest overall verdict: not ready now, ready when the named items close\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen running a readiness review, audit each prior artifact against its criterion first, assign go, no-go, or conditional second, and refuse any shortcut that reverses a boundary third. Before you treat the review as finished, confirm it answers:\n\n1. **Traceability check:** Does each verdict point to a specific prior artifact and its criterion?\n2. **Blocker check:** Is any safety, consent, or equity item a no-go that must close before launch?\n3. **Integrity check:** Did you record any tempting shortcut you rejected, and why it would reverse a boundary?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\n<inline-check question=\"A reviewer writes: 'Seven of eight items pass, so we are ready to launch,' even though the eighth is unsent consent. What error is this?\" explanation=\"Averaging the verdicts. Readiness is not a majority vote; a single safety, consent, or equity no-go blocks the launch regardless of how many other items are green.\" options='[{\"text\":\"No error; a strong majority means ready.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Averaging verdicts, which lets a single blocking no-go be outvoted.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Failing to add a polished summary.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Citing too many prior artifacts.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe most damaging error is treating the review as a formality whose only acceptable outcome is approval. If the review cannot return a no-go, it is not protecting anyone. A second error is averaging the verdicts, declaring overall readiness because most items passed; readiness is not a majority vote, and a single consent failure blocks the launch regardless of how many other items are green. A third error is accepting a plausible shortcut, such as gathering consent after launch, which trades a real boundary for convenience.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nImagine you are the teacher about to deploy an AI reading tutor. Pull each prior artifact and judge it against its own criterion: is the tension named, is the tool bounded with a rejection, does the rubric score judgment, does the memo answer consent and name a fallback, does the evidence plan have a baseline? Assign go, no-go, or conditional to each, citing the section it came from. When you hit the unsent consent forms, write no-go and stop the launch clock; when you hit the uncollected baseline, write conditional with a deadline. Then state the overall verdict honestly: not ready now, ready when the named items close.\n\nThe artifact is the review itself: a verdict per prior section, each traceable, an honest overall verdict, and at least one recorded rejection of a boundary-reversing shortcut.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead a readiness review by asking whether it could ever say no. If every line trends toward approval, it is a celebration, not a gate. Check that each verdict cites a specific artifact and that a single blocker is allowed to stop the whole launch. Keep a two-column note: the prior artifact and its criterion on the left, the verdict and its evidence on the right. A strong review lets an administrator see exactly what is ready, what is blocked, and why launch is or is not safe this week.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA readiness review meets the standard when a mentor teacher can see a traceable verdict for each prior artifact, an honest overall verdict that a blocker can override, and a recorded rejection of any boundary-reversing shortcut. Presentation is the last layer; the per-artifact verdicts and the willingness to fail are load-bearing. If a verdict has no cited artifact, annotate the gap and ask BigAL to compare your review against an exemplar that returns a no-go rather than to make the summary sound more confident. A useful closing line reads: \"I tied each verdict to a prior artifact and held a no-go on unsent consent, and I rejected the suggestion to launch first and collect consent later because it reverses the policy boundary.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the readiness review from a single classroom to a school-wide rollout. The gate logic transfers, but the blockers scale: consent must be tracked across many sections, the fallback must work in rooms with different device access, and the evidence baseline must be comparable across teachers. A single building-level no-go, such as one grade lacking consent, can hold the whole rollout. Rebuilding the review for the school-wide case, rather than reusing the single-class version, shows you can run readiness as a gate at any scale.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/08-08-readiness-review-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"08.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)** (Mishra & Koehler, 2006; for AI-rich settings, Ning et al., 2024).\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\nBehind these prompts sit four backend rail actions, each with a visible counterpart above: *explain* powers the concise explanation offered for a question note, *represent* powers the weak-versus-strong comparison offered for a confusion note, *practice* powers the transfer of the decision rule offered for an insight note, and *ask* powers the follow-up on where a connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Design Tension Studio map naming a classroom value conflict and negotiated decision for CAT 531 08.08. A peer comment points to an interface move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What would make this section more useful while you revise?\" options='[\"It asks you to name a decision, point to evidence, and choose support that helps the revision.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_readiness_review\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\", \"cat531_supplement_readiness_review\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure your review assigns a traceable verdict to each prior artifact, lets a single safety or consent blocker hold the launch, and records at least one boundary-reversing shortcut you refused.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- Ning, Y., Zhang, C., Xu, B., Zhou, Y., & Wijaya, T. T. (2024). Teachers' AI-TPACK: Exploring the relationship between knowledge elements. *Sustainability, 16*(3), 978. https://doi.org/10.3390/su16030978\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315670160\n- Perrotta, C., & Selwyn, N. (2020). Deep learning goes to school: Toward a relational understanding of AI in education. [*Learning, Media and Technology*](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- Mecoli, S. (2013). The influence of the pedagogical content knowledge theoretical framework on research on preservice teacher education. *Journal of Education, 193*(3). https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_review_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_readiness_review","stem":"A readiness review reads: \"Everything looks good, we are ready to launch the AI tutor next week.\" What makes this a weak review?","options":["It is too optimistic about students.","It is a rubber stamp: it does not check each prior artifact against a go or no-go criterion.","It names a specific tool.","It mentions a launch date."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A readiness review is a gate. Asserting readiness without an item-by-item check skips the actual work.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_rubber_stamp","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531","cat531_supplement_readiness_review","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_review_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"During the review the consent forms for the AI tutor have not gone home. What should the readiness verdict be on that item?","options":["Go, because consent can be handled later.","No-go on that item: launch is blocked until opt-out paths and the low-tech fallback are in place.","Conditional, because consent is optional.","Go, because the rest of the items passed."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A gate must be able to fail. An unmet blocking item like unsent consent forms returns a no-go.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_cannot_fail","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_readiness_review","evidence_based_revision"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_review_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"Someone proposes launching the AI tutor now and gathering consent afterward. Why is this not acceptable?","options":["It would take too long to write the memo.","It reverses the policy boundary: consent and the low-tech fallback must be in place before any launch.","It would make the review shorter.","Launching early is fine if students agree."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Launch-then-comply reverses the consent boundary. The review must reject it.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_launch_then_comply","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_readiness_review","ai_supported_learning"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_review_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which readiness verdict is most auditable by a mentor teacher?","options":["We are ready overall.","Go on the tension map (08.01), tool-boundary transcript (08.02), and rubric (08.04); no-go on policy until consent and the 08.06 fallback are in place; conditional on collecting the 08.07 baseline before week 1.","Most things are done.","The team feels confident."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The strongest verdict ties each go, no-go, and conditional to the specific prior artifact that justifies it.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_no_artifact_tie","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_readiness_review","evidence_based_revision"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_rubber_stamp","pattern":"treating_the_review_as_a_rubber_stamp","description":"Declaring readiness as a celebration that everything looks good rather than checking each prior artifact against a go or no-go criterion","trigger":"review asserts readiness with no item-by-item check","feedback":"Treat readiness as a gate that can fail. Check each prior artifact against a go or no-go criterion.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_cannot_fail","pattern":"a_review_that_cannot_return_no_go","description":"Running a readiness review that has no real failure condition, so a launch can never be blocked even when consent or policy is incomplete","trigger":"review has no criterion that would stop a launch","feedback":"Hold a no-go when a blocking item (such as unsent consent forms) is unmet. A gate must be able to fail.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_no_artifact_tie","pattern":"verdict_not_tied_to_a_prior_artifact","description":"Giving a readiness verdict with no reference to the specific section artifacts (tension map, transcript, rubric, policy memo, evidence plan) that justify it","trigger":"verdict cites no prior artifact as the basis","feedback":"Tie each go or no-go to the specific prior artifact that supports it so the verdict is auditable.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_8_readiness_launch_then_comply","pattern":"launching_first_and_complying_later","description":"Accepting a plan to launch the AI tool now and gather consent or collect the baseline afterward, reversing the policy boundary","trigger":"review approves launch before consent and baseline are in place","feedback":"Do not launch before consent and baseline close. Reject plans that reverse the policy boundary.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"1f59b88912f6e34b3119c6de1965cc6a5346ce678c5fddad80895bd92ec832d4","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}